const TOPICS = {
  "TOPIC_01": {
    "name": "Software Inflation",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_01\nTOPIC_NAME: Software Inflation\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 8, 9, 10\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: None\n\nCore ideas:\n• Software inflation is the progressive growth of the software estate through applications, subscriptions, licences and consumption services.\n• Direct drivers include price, volume, add-on, portfolio and consumption inflation.\n• Scope expansion must be separated from like-for-like price inflation.\n• Microsoft standardised affected EA and MPSA online-service pricing across Levels A to D from 1 November 2025. Loss of an automatic volume-level discount is customer-specific and must be calculated from the former net EA price.\n• Microsoft increased the Microsoft 365 E3 list price from $36 to $39 per user per month effective 1 July 2026.\n• Bundling can create genuine cost avoidance, but only where included entitlements replace separately purchased products and meet the required use case.\n\nPractical value:\n• Establish the previous contracted net-price baseline before calculating renewal inflation.\n• Separate price inflation from scope-expansion inflation.\n• Review adoption and remove shelfware.\n• Segment licences by role.\n• Use included entitlements before purchasing add-ons.\n• Validate bundle rights, eligibility and functional limitations.\n• Apply consumption quotas and budget caps.\n• Preserve renewal leverage by monitoring dependency and switching costs.\n\nExample used on slide 10:\n• Pre-July E3 baseline: 2,000 × $36 × 12 = $864,000.\n• Post-July E3 cost: 2,000 × $39 × 12 = $936,000.\n• Price and packaging inflation: $72,000, or 8.3%.\n• Scope-expansion inflation: $217,800, or 25.2%.\n• Overall software inflation: $289,800, or 33.5%.\n• Confirmed bundle avoidance: $20,400, reducing overall inflation to 31.2%.\n• Conditional Power Automate avoidance: another $27,000, reducing overall inflation to 28.1% if bundled rights meet requirements.\n\nBlog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/microsoft-ai-licensing-understanding-through-practice\n\nExternal:\nhttps://www.microsoft.com/en-us/licensing/news/online-services-pricing-consistency-update\nhttps://www.microsoft.com/en-us/licensing/news/2026-m365-packaging-pricing-updates\nhttps://www.vertice.one/l/saas-inflation-index-report\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services"
  },
  "TOPIC_02": {
    "name": "Digitalization Paradox",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_02\nTOPIC_NAME: Digitalization Paradox\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 15\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nTechnology ≠ Transformation\nTransformation without adoption and measurable outcomes is likely a waste of funds.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/08/digital-transformation-diminishing-returns.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.distributed.com/blog/gartner-says-80-of-it-projects-fail-but-not-with-distributed-s-elastic-teams\n5. Practical value:\nApply the full P.O.W.E.R. model\nDon’t buy technology as a solution (don’t start with E in P.O.W.E.R.)\nLink contracts to adoption & outcomes\nUse POC → scale (incremental development and financing)\nAvoid “promised value” – separate legacy from innovation; measure outcomes separately.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP implemented → old processes maintained through customization → no value\nAI deployed → no adoption → no usage\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: None explicitly identified in the source topic block.\nASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE: P.O.W.E.R. model of digital transformation — Processes; Operating model; Workforce (People); Enablement; Results"
  },
  "TOPIC_03": {
    "name": "SaaS Market Reality & Trends",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_03\nTOPIC_NAME: SaaS Market Reality & Trends\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 7, 11, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nSaaS growth ≠ SaaS efficiency\nSaaS market operates paper-thin margins → software inflation + upsell\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/10/the-saas-market-and-it-procurement-350.html\n4. External:\nhttps://zylo.com/reports/2026-saas-management-index/\nhttps://www.vertice.one/l/saas-inflation-index-report\nhttps://www.highalpha.com/saas-benchmarks\n5. Practical value:\nTreat SaaS as a portfolio\nControl software inflation\nEliminate:\nshelfware\nredundant apps\nmulti-channel spend\nshadow IT spend\nChallenge:\nauto-renewals\nhidden pricing\nunbudgeted/unapproved spend\nShift:\nlicenses → usage → outcomes → value\n\nEXAMPLES:\n275 apps per company → scope overlap → waste\nAuto-renewal +9% → uncontrolled software inflation\nAI hype → bundle and upsell → budget overrun\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_02 — Digitalization Paradox; TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse; TOPIC_01 — Software Inflation\nASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE: SaaS Vendor Economics; AI vs SaaS"
  },
  "TOPIC_04": {
    "name": "Servitization of IT",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_04\nTOPIC_NAME: Servitization of IT\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 14, 17\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: None\n\nCore ideas:\n• Servitization shifts IT procurement from buying isolated products or services to sourcing integrated solutions.\n• Product value is created mainly through production and operations before exchange, then realised at purchase or use.\n• Service value unfolds over time through use, outcomes, adaptability and the evolving buyer–supplier relationship.\n• Integrated solutions combine products and services, so value depends on continuing performance rather than the initial transaction alone.\n• Competitive advantage increasingly comes from a supplier's ability to provide and support the integrated solution.\n\nPractical value:\n• Evaluate lifecycle outcomes, availability, continuity and supplier expertise.\n• Treat governance, adaptability and relationship quality as part of value creation.\n• Measure realised value during service use rather than relying only on product features or the initial purchase price.\n• Contract for service performance, ongoing improvement and effective buyer–supplier collaboration.\n• Assess whether the integrated solution creates more value than separately purchased components.\n\nExamples:\n• Anything as a Service (XaaS)\n• AI as a Service (AIaaS)\n• A technology platform combined with implementation, support, upgrades and continuous optimisation\n\nBlog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/03/value-procurement-framework.html\n\nExternal:\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/0199263221.003.0016\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/17/19/8756\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_02 — Digitalization Paradox; TOPIC_11 — NIST Cloud Computing Model; TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends"
  },
  "TOPIC_05": {
    "name": "Digitization vs Digitalization",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_05\nTOPIC_NAME: Digitization vs Digitalization\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 15\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nDigitization = converting analog information into digital form\nDigitalization = using digital technologies to improve or redesign processes\nDigitization is a technical step; digitalization is a business transformation\n3. Blog:\nN/A\n4. External:\nhttps://www.sap.com/resources/digitization-vs-digitalization\n5. Practical value:\nDo not confuse data conversion with transformation\nDigitization alone does not create value\nFocus on process change and adoption, not just systems\nUse digitization as an input to digitalization initiatives\n\nEXAMPLES:\nScanning paper contracts → digitization\nImplementing workflow automation on top → digitalization\nConverting invoices to PDF → digitization\nAutomating invoice approval and matching → digitalization\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_02 — Digitalization Paradox"
  },
  "TOPIC_06": {
    "name": "Bimodal IT",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_06\nTOPIC_NAME: Bimodal IT\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 18, 19, 20\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT operates in two parallel modes:\n – Mode 1: stability, reliability, efficiency\n – Mode 2: agility, experimentation, innovation\nThe model separates core systems from innovation initiatives\nDesigned to balance risk control vs speed of change\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/bimodal-procurement-vs-adaptive-sourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.gartner.com/en/information-technology/glossary/bimodal\n5. Practical value:\nDo not apply generic governance to all IT initiatives\nSeparate sourcing strategies:\nMode 1 → long-term, stable, cost-optimized contracts\nMode 2 → flexible, short-term, experimental sourcing\nAlign procurement with:\nrisk tolerance\ncontract lifespan and supplier relationships\nbusiness impact\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP system → Mode 1 (stability, control) → long-term contract with a strategic supplier\nAI pilot / analytics tool → Mode 2 (experimentation, speed) → short-term contract based on the POC outcome with a no-name supplier\nCore infrastructure outsourcing → Mode 1\nStartup SaaS tool trial → Mode 2\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_08 — Pace Layer Model; TOPIC_07 — Buygrid Framework"
  },
  "TOPIC_07": {
    "name": "Buygrid Framework",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_07\nTOPIC_NAME: Buygrid Framework\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 21, 26\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThe Buygrid Framework explains how organizations make purchasing decisions\nIt combines:\nBuy phases (steps in the process)\nBuy classes (type of purchase situation)\nDecision complexity depends on:\nNovelty\nrisk\nvalue.\n3. Blog\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2022/06/the-new-look-on-industrial-buying.html\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/09/it-procurement-vs-legacy-sourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2012/04/9802_The_BuyGrid_Model_30_Years.pdf\n5. Practical value:\nAdjust sourcing strategy based on buy class:\nNew Task → high effort, exploration, stakeholder alignment\nModified Rebuy → optimization, negotiation\nStraight Rebuy → efficiency, automation\nAvoid using a single approach for all purchases\n\nEXAMPLES:\nFirst-time ERP implementation → New Task\nSwitching cloud provider → Modified Rebuy\nRenewing SaaS licenses → Straight Rebuy\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix"
  },
  "TOPIC_08": {
    "name": "Pace Layer Model",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_08\nTOPIC_NAME: Pace Layer Model\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 22, 23, 24, 26\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT systems evolve at different speeds and must be managed accordingly\nThree layers:\nSystems of Record → stable, core processes, data, and transactions\nSystems of Differentiation → support advanced business processes and competitive positioning, enable current competitive advantage\nSystems of Innovation → experimental, rapidly changing solutions, provide future competitive advantage.\nEach layer requires a different:\ngovernance model\nsourcing approach\ninvestment horizon\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/bimodal-procurement-vs-adaptive-sourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.gartner.com/en/information-technology/glossary/pace-layer-application-strategy\nhttps://cio-wiki.org/wiki/Gartner%27s_PACE_Layered_Application_Strategy\n5. Practical value:\nDo not apply uniform sourcing across all systems\nAlign procurement strategy with system layer:\nSystems of Record → stability, long-term contracts, risk control\nSystems of Differentiation → flexibility, medium-term, best-of-breed “beauty contest”\nSystems of Innovation → speed, experimentation, short cycles\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP / financial systems → Systems of Record\nCRM customization → Systems of Differentiation\nAI pilot tools → Systems of Innovation\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix"
  },
  "TOPIC_09": {
    "name": "ITIL Service Lifecycle",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_09\nTOPIC_NAME: ITIL Service Lifecycle\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 27, 28, 33, 57, 58\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nITIL defines how IT services are designed, delivered, and improved across their lifecycle. It starts from the Business/Customer requirements, demonstrating IT’s dedication to the business.\nFive lifecycle stages:\nService Strategy → define budget, demand, and service portfolio\nService Design → design services, processes, and SLAs\nService Transition → build, test, and deploy services\nService Operation → deliver and support services\nContinual Service Improvement (CSI) → measure and enhance performance\nFocus shifts from systems to end-to-end service value.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/05/procurement-40-adaptivity-beyond-agility.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.axelos.com/best-practice-solutions/itil\n5. Practical value:\nProcurement must align with lifecycle stage:\nStrategy → define sourcing model, make/buy decisions\nDesign → embed SLAs, KPIs, and service levels in contracts\nTransition → manage vendor onboarding and risk\nOperation → monitor performance and enforce SLAs\nCSI → drive continuous improvement and renegotiation\nAvoid:\nfocusing only on purchase (design)\nignoring lifecycle costs, performance, and project rollout plan\n\nEXAMPLES:\nOutsourcing IT support:\nStrategy → define scope\nDesign → SLA structure\nTransition → migration\nOperation → service delivery\nCSI → performance improvement\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service"
  },
  "TOPIC_10": {
    "name": "TCO of a Technology Service",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_10\nTOPIC_NAME: TCO of a Technology Service\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 25, 31, 32, 33, 57, 58\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 32\n\n2. Core ideas:\nTCO is mapped across the ITIL Service Lifecycle\nCosts are not static — they occur across Strategy → Design → Transition → Operation → End-of-life\nTCO is a function of decisions, not a fixed number.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/07/opportunity-cost-vs-tco-in-outsourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.meegle.com/en_us/topics/it-service/tco-total-cost-of-ownership-in-it\n5. Practical value:\nTCO of a technology service may include:\nService Strategy\nCost of discovery (business analysis)\nMarket research\nSupplier evaluation\nPOC / MVP / prototype\nRelationship building\nNegotiations\nService Design\nCost of service design\nSupplier selection\nContract negotiation\nSupplier audit\nScoping\nService Transition\nSetup (cloud / infrastructure)\nCustomization\nHardware / software acquisition\nDeployment and migration\nTesting\nLegacy decommissioning\nProject management\\\nChange management\nKnowledge management\nService Operation\nCloud services\nSoftware rental\nNetwork costs\nChange requests (incl. renegotiation)\nSupport and maintenance:\nPreventative / corrective (SaaS)\nPerfective / adaptive (ad-hoc)\nUpgrades (software / firmware)\nAsset management\nSRM\nCompliance (technical, legal, environmental)\nRisk management\nEnd-of-life\nRetirement / replacement costs\nEnvironmental costs\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP cost is not $80k–$120k, but ranges from $3k → $1M+ depending on a solution depth, scale, integrations, customization, teams, and decision-making.\nBritish Airways case:\n£27.5M savings vs £150M failure impact\nTCO considerations must include risk exposure and opportunity cost.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_09 — ITIL Service Lifecycle; TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix"
  },
  "TOPIC_11": {
    "name": "NIST Cloud Computing Model",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_11\nTOPIC_NAME: NIST Cloud Computing Model\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 35, 36\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\n“Cloud computing is a model for enabling ubiquitous, convenient, on-demand network access to a shared pool of configurable computing resources … that can be rapidly provisioned and released with minimal management effort or service provider interaction.”\n3. Blog\n4. External:\nhttps://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-145.pdf\n5. Practical value:\nThe most neglected characteristics in practice:\non-demand self-service\nrapid elasticity\nProcurement implication:\nDo not assume “cloud” = compliant with NIST model\nValidate:\nability to scale down (not only up)\nreal usage-based charging\nindependence from provider intervention\n\nEXAMPLES:\nSaaS with no scale-down → violates elasticity → shelfware occurs.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_12 — Cloud Deployment Models; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services"
  },
  "TOPIC_12": {
    "name": "Cloud Deployment Models",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_12\nTOPIC_NAME: Cloud Deployment Models\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 38\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nCloud deployment defines where and how infrastructure is operated and accessed\nFour deployment models:\nPublic cloud\nprovider-owned infrastructure available to the general public\nPrivate cloud\nenvironment dedicated to a single organization\nHybrid cloud\ncombination of public/private integrated with on-premise systems\nCommunity cloud\nshared infrastructure used by multiple organizations with common requirements.\n3. Blog:\n4. External:\nhttps://www.flackbox.com/cloud-deployment-models-public-private-hybrid-cloud\n5. Practical value:\nDeployment choice impacts:\ndata protection\ncost structure\ncontrol vs flexibility\nPublic cloud:\nscalability, accessibility\nlower control and security but lower cost\nPrivate cloud:\nhigh control and security\nhigher cost\nHybrid cloud:\nbalance of flexibility and control\nintegration complexity\nCommunity cloud:\nshared compliance environment\ncost sharing across organizations with similar needs.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nPublic → SaaS platforms\nPrivate → internal regulated systems\nHybrid → sensitive data on-prem + scalable workloads in cloud\nCommunity → government / regulated sector shared infrastructure\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_11 — NIST Cloud Computing Model; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services"
  },
  "TOPIC_13": {
    "name": "Cloud Services",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_13\nTOPIC_NAME: Cloud Services\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 37, 39\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nCloud services are structured in three service models:\nIaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)\nbasic compute, storage, and network resources\nPaaS (Platform as a Service)\non-demand application-hosting environment\nSaaS (Software as a Service)\nready-to-use cloud applications\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/09/cost-and-performance-tradeoff-with-multiobjective-analysis.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.redhat.com/en/topics/cloud-computing/what-are-cloud-services\n5. Practical value:\nService model determines:\nlevel of control\nresponsibility split\ncost structure\nIaaS:\nmaximum control\nhigher internal management effort\nPaaS:\nreduced infrastructure management\nfocus on application development\nSaaS:\nminimal management effort\nfastest deployment\nKey sourcing implication:\nThe higher the service layer → the less control, but the faster the deployment\n\nEXAMPLES:\nIaaS → virtual machines, storage\nPaaS → development platforms, databases\nSaaS → CRM, ERP cloud applications\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_11 — NIST Cloud Computing Model; TOPIC_12 — Cloud Deployment Models; TOPIC_27 — Technology Procurement Framework"
  },
  "TOPIC_14": {
    "name": "Buy or Build",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_14\nTOPIC_NAME: Buy or Build\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 41, 42\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nOrganizations choose between:\nBuy (COTS — Commercial Off-The-Shelf)\nready-made solutions acquired from the market\nBuild (SDLC — Software Development Lifecycle)\ninternally developed solutions following stages:\nPlan → Define → Design → Build → Test → Deploy → Maintain\nBuild (development) methodologies:\nWaterfall\nsequential, plan-driven, fixed-scope\nAgile\niterative, incremental delivery, incremental scope\nvalue delivered in sprints\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/12/modern-dynamics-of-saas-procurement-negotiations.html\n4. External:\nhttps://productschool.com/blog/leadership/build-vs-buy\n5. Practical value:\nDecision is not binary → third option exists:\nCustomize (hybrid approach)\nKey decision drivers:\nBuy (COTS):\nfaster deployment\nlower development effort\nlimited differentiation\nBuild (SDLC):\ntailored to business needs\nhigher cost and complexity\nlonger time to value\nProcurement implication:\nIT procurement is not only about buying — it is about structuring the optimal delivery model. In fact, Buy or Build is a typical category management questions, so don’t always rush into sourcing.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nBuy → SaaS CRM solution\nBuild → custom solution on an industrial PaaS (e.g., Azure)\nHybrid → SaaS + custom integrations for third-party or self-developed solutions. Heavily-customized COTS is the worst version of hybrid.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_12 — Cloud Deployment Models"
  },
  "TOPIC_15": {
    "name": "Horizontal, Vertical, and Nano-SaaS",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_15\nTOPIC_NAME: Horizontal, Vertical, and Nano-SaaS\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 46\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nHorizontal SaaS\ngeneral-purpose solutions used across industries\nVertical SaaS\nindustry-specific solutions tailored to a particular sector\nNano / Micro SaaS\nultra-specialised applications solving a single narrow task\nStrategic positioning:\nHorizontal SaaS → broad cost leadership / scale\nVertical SaaS → differentiation / deep industry expertise\nNano-SaaS → micro-services / differentiation focus\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/nano-saas-your-personal-digital-tranformation\n4. External:\nhttps://flgpartners.com/saas-industry-centric-business-models-horizontal-vertical/\n5. Practical value:\nHorizontal SaaS:\nleverage competition\nbenchmark pricing\npush for modular and flexible pricing\nVertical SaaS:\njustify premium pricing via domain expertise\nsecure:\nintegration support\nuptime guarantees\nroadmap influence\nNano / Micro SaaS:\nfast adoption\nhigh fragmentation risk\ncontributes to:\nshadow IT/vibe coding\nredundant apps\n\nEXAMPLES:\nHorizontal → CRM, collaboration tools\nVertical → banking SaaS, healthcare SaaS\nNano → single-purpose tools (e.g. niche AI apps)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services; TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse"
  },
  "TOPIC_16": {
    "name": "The SaaSpocalypse",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_16\nTOPIC_NAME: The SaaSpocalypse\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nSaaS market is shifting under pressure from:\nPricing model transformation\nconsumption-based\nvalue-based\nhybrid pricing models\nEconomic pressure on SaaS vendors:\nlow net profit margins (~1.5–2%)\nhigh customer acquisition cost\nlong payback period (~22 months)\nShift in revenue strategy:\nfocus on existing customers (retention & expansion)\nupsell hyped services (AI) to improve overall outlook\nAI as a threat:\nfight accelerates for data, control, and orchestration layer\nincreasing pressure from AI agents and vibe coding\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/from-telco-dumb-pipes-to-ai-orchestrated-software\n4. External:\nhttps://www.thesaascfo.com/the-saaspocalypse-ai-agents-vibe-coding-and-the-changing-economics-of-saas/\n5. Practical value:\nConsumption-based model\nsuited for variable demand\nrisk: uncontrolled cost growth\nValue-based model\nsuited for differentiated solutions\nrequires clear ROI linkage\nSubscription-based model\npredictable cost\nrisk of overcommitment\nKey SaaS negotiation logic:\nemphasize retention value\nanalyse CLTV (Customer Lifetime Value)\npush for benefits as a long-term customer\nalign pricing with actual usage and value delivery\ndon’t accept “promised value”\n\nEXAMPLES:\nVendor pushes consumption pricing → buyer must control usage\nLong-term contracts → leverage retention economics\nAI monetization → unclear value vs cost\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends"
  },
  "TOPIC_17": {
    "name": "SaaS Pricing Models",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_17\nTOPIC_NAME: SaaS Pricing Models\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 40, 43, 50, 83\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\n• Common pricing models include:\n  o Seat-based pricing\n  o Consumption-based pricing\n  o Value-based pricing\n  o Tiered pricing\n  o Flat-rate subscription\n• Each model allocates commercial risk differently between customer and supplier.\n3. Blog:\nN/A\n4. External:\nhttps://www.paddle.com/blog/saas-pricing-models-strategies-fltr\n5. Practical value:\n• Match the pricing model to demand predictability.\n• Seat-based pricing offers budget certainty but creates shelfware risk.\n• Consumption-based pricing improves flexibility but requires strong governance.\n• Value-based pricing aligns cost with business outcomes but requires measurable value.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n• Microsoft 365 – seat-based\n• Azure OpenAI – consumption-based\n• Procurement analytics platform priced on managed spend – value-based\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service; TOPIC_27 — Technology Procurement Framework"
  },
  "TOPIC_18": {
    "name": "Types of IT Outsourcing Services",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_18\nTOPIC_NAME: Types of IT Outsourcing Services\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 61\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThere are at least four main outsourcing models:\nStaff Augmentation\ntemporary resources to cover skill or capacity gaps\nIT Outsourcing (end-to-end)\noutsourcing of complete teams, functions, or critical services\nManaged Services\ninfrastructure-centric outsourcing of highly complex business processes or critical services\nAs-a-Service\nstandardized, cloud-based, application-centric services.\nKey dimensions across models:\nControl → highest in staff augmentation, lowest in as-a-service\nSpeed (to mobilize) → fastest in as-a-service, slowest in large outsourcing\nContract type →\nrate card / T&M (time & material)\nproject fee / retainer\nSLA-based long-term agreements\nsubscription agreements\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/deep-dive-into-outsourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://kruschecompany.com/it-outsourcing-types/\n5. Practical value:\nSelection depends on:\nurgency of need\ncomplexity of service\nrequired control\nstrategic importance\nTypical use cases:\nStaff augmentation:\nimmediate resource gaps\nManaged services:\noutsourcing defined operational processes\nIT outsourcing:\ncomplex or critical services\nlong-term strategic delivery\nAs-a-Service:\nstandardized, automated cloud solutions\nCritical limitation of outsourcing:\nDo NOT outsource activities that define competitive advantage, critical data, or core decision-making\n\nEXAMPLES:\nStaff augmentation → hiring developers via T&M\nIT outsourcing → outsourced IT support\nManaged services → data transmission network operation and maintenance\nAs-a-Service → SaaS / cloud-based platforms (e.g., SAP Concur)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service"
  },
  "TOPIC_19": {
    "name": "Outsourcing Delivery Models",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_19\nTOPIC_NAME: Outsourcing Delivery Models\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 63, 64\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nDelivery models define where and how services are delivered geographically\nMain models:\nOnsite / Landed\nresources located at the client’s premises\nOnshore\ndelivery within the same country\nNearshore\ndelivery from a neighbouring or nearby country\nOffshore\ndelivery from a distant country (typically low-cost locations)\nCore trade-off:\nCost vs control vs proximity\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/deep-dive-into-outsourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://orases.com/blog/the-differences-between-onshore-offshore-software-development/\n5. Practical value:\nOnsite / Landed:\nhighest cost\nhighest control and communication quality\nOnshore:\nhigh cost\nstrong alignment with business and regulation\nNearshore:\nbalanced cost\nmanageable time zone and cultural alignment\nOffshore:\nlowest cost\nhighest coordination complexity\nlower direct control\nCritical sourcing implication:\nallocate activities based on value:\nhigh-value / strategic → onsite / onshore\nexecution / scalable tasks → nearshore / offshore\n\nEXAMPLES:\nOnsite → project management, architecture\nNearshore → development teams\nOffshore → testing, support (L1/L2/L3)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_18 — Types of IT Outsourcing Services; TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service"
  },
  "TOPIC_20": {
    "name": "ISO 19770-1:2017 Framework of ITAM Processes",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_20\nTOPIC_NAME: ISO 19770-1:2017 Framework of ITAM Processes\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 68, 69\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 70\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT Asset Management (ITAM) is structured as a management system aligned to lifecycle and governance\nThree core pillars of ITAM:\n1 – Trustworthy Data\naccurate, complete, and reliable asset data\n2 – Lifecycle Integration\nassets managed across full lifecycle: Plan → Acquire/Develop → Maintain → Retire\n3 – Optimization\ncontinuous improvement and cost/value optimization.\nManagement system structure (PDCA cycle):\nPlan → Do → Check → Act\nembedded across ITAM processes\nCore process areas:\nContract management\nFinancial management\nVendor management\nLicense management\nSecurity management\nData management\nChange management\nService level & risk management\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/01/5-solutions-to-increase-saas-savings-in-procurement.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.iso.org/standard/68531.html\n5. Practical value:\nITAM is a foundation for IT procurement control:\nenables:\ncost savings\ncost avoidance\ncompliance\ncontrol\nProcurement implication:\nWithout ITAM → no visibility → no control over licenses, assets, and spend\n\nEXAMPLES:\nLicense audit (SAP / Oracle) → requires accurate usage data\nAsset lifecycle tracking → prevents over-purchasing\nVendor management integration → aligns contracts with actual usage\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service"
  },
  "TOPIC_21": {
    "name": "Kraljic Matrix",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_21\nTOPIC_NAME: Kraljic Matrix\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 71\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThe Kraljic Matrix classifies purchases based on:\nProfit impact (value / business importance)\nSupply risk (market complexity / level of competition/ availability of goods/services)\nFour Quadrants:\nNon-critical items\nlow value, low risk\nLeverage items\nhigh value, low risk\nBottleneck items\nlow value, high risk\nStrategic items\nhigh value, high risk\nTypical sourcing strategies:\nNon-critical:\nstandardize\nconsolidate\nefficient buying channels\nLeverage:\nexploit competition\nnegotiate aggressively\nuse market power\nBottleneck:\nsecure supply\nreduce dependency\nmanage risk\nStrategic:\nlong-term partnerships\nperformance incentives\njoint planning (e.g., CPFR)\nBlog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/09/game-theory-and-procurement-strategic.html\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/01/adding-new-dimensions-to-kraljic-matrix.html\n4. External:\nhttps://hbr.org/1983/09/purchasing-must-become-supply-management\n5. Practical value:\nDo NOT apply a single sourcing strategy to all categories\nAlign procurement approach with:\nsupply market complexity\nbusiness impact\nIT-specific implication:\nStrategic → ERP, core platforms\nLeverage → standard software, hardware\nBottleneck → niche technologies\nNon-critical → routine IT commodities.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP system → Strategic\nsoftware licenses with many vendors → Leverage\nniche cybersecurity tool → Bottleneck\nstandard hardware → Non-critical.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_07 — Buygrid Framework"
  },
  "TOPIC_22": {
    "name": "Sourcing Value Levers",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_22\nTOPIC_NAME: Sourcing Value Levers\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 30, 67, 72\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES:None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nValue levers are structured ways to create value in sourcing\nThey operate across multiple dimensions of a deal, not only price\nGroups of core value levers:\nDemand management\nSpecification / scope optimisation\nCommercial model design\nSupplier competition / selection\nContract structure and terms\nPerformance management\nSupplier relationship management\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/01/saas-negotiation-strategies.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.kearney.com/insights/books/the-purchasing-chessboard/levers\n5. Practical value:\nValue is created by designing the sourcing process, not just negotiating\nKey principle: Each lever targets a different part of the value chain.\nWhat’s you permission to buy\nwhat you buy vs. what you need\nhow you buy\nfrom whom\nunder what terms\nhow it performs\nCritical implication:\nPrice = only one of many sourcing value levers\nOver-focusing on price → value destruction\n\nEXAMPLES:\nDemand management → reduce SaaS license consumption\nCommercial model → shift to consumption pricing\nContract → introduce true-up/down\nPerformance → SLA + service credits\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix; TOPIC_07 — Buygrid Framework; TOPIC_27 — Technology Procurement Framework"
  },
  "TOPIC_23": {
    "name": "Information Asymmetry",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_23\nTOPIC_NAME: Information Asymmetry\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 70, 73, 74, 75\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 71\n\n2. Core ideas:\nInformation asymmetry occurs when one party possesses more or better information than the other\nIn procurement, suppliers often know more about:\ncosts\ntechnical complexity\nimplementation risks\nservice capability\nInformation asymmetry creates sourcing and governance risks across the contract lifecycle\n\nClassic forms of information asymmetry:\nAdverse selection (BEFORE THE DEAL)\nsupplier overstates its capability before contract award\nbuyer cannot fully distinguish between good and bad suppliers\nMoral hazard (AFTER THE DEAL)\nsupplier behavior deteriorates after contract award\nhidden actions and reduced effort become difficult to observe\nPrincipal-agent problem\no agent incentives diverge from the principal objectives\no advisors or integrators may optimize their own interests instead of the buyer value\n3. Blog\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/10/information-asymmetry-in-saas-negotiations.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/economics-econometrics-and-finance/information-asymmetry\n5. Practical value:\nConduct supplier due diligence\nValidate supplier claims through references and benchmarking\nUse:\nKPIs\nSLAs\ngovernance structures\noutcome-based contracts\nReduce dependency on opaque supplier-controlled information\nAlign incentives to reduce opportunistic behavior.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nSupplier intentionally “low-balls” implementation costs during tender\nSaaS vendor hides future scaling and support costs\nIntegrator maximizes billable hours instead of long-term customer value\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_26 — Relational Contracts; TOPIC_24 — Types of Negotiations and Game Theory"
  },
  "TOPIC_24": {
    "name": "Types of Negotiations and Game Theory",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_24\nTOPIC_NAME: Types of Negotiations and Game Theory\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 55, 70, 74, 76\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 56, 71\n\n2. Core ideas:\nTwo fundamental types of negotiations:\nDistributive negotiations (= fixed pie, win-lose, zero-sum games)\nFocus: claiming value\nOne party’s gain = other party’s loss\nNo expansion of total value\nIntegrative negotiations (= win-win, cooperative games)\nFocus: creating value\nExpand the “pie” before dividing it\nMutual benefit possible\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/integrative-and-distributive-negotiations\n4. External:\nhttps://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/negotiation-skills-daily/negotiation-skills-expanding-the-pie-integrative-bargaining-versus-distributive-bargaining/\n5. Practical value:\nIntegrative (cooperative) tactics:\nInterest exploration\nInformation sharing\nTrust building\nJoint problem-solving\nCreating multiple options\nDistributive (zero-sum) tactics:\nAggressive anchoring\nClaiming value\nWithholding information\nUsing leverage\nMapping to Kraljic:\nLeverage / Non-critical → Distributive\nStrategic / Bottleneck → Integrative\n\nEXAMPLES:\nSaaS renewal with many vendors → distributive (price pressure)\nERP transformation → integrative (value + risk sharing)\nGame theory\nNegotiation = strategic interaction between players\nOutcomes depend on:\nyour actions\ncounterparty actions\nNash Equilibrium→ no party benefits from changing strategy unilaterally\nExample:\nConsultant chooses between:\nbest supplier (value for company)\nsuboptimal supplier (personal gain)\nSolution:\nincentive-based contract aligns interests\nequilibrium achieved when both maximise payoff\nNegotiation is a game:\nDistributive = fight for value\nIntegrative = create value\nGame theory = design the rules so the “right” outcome becomes rational.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_23 — Information Asymmetry; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix; TOPIC_22 — Sourcing Value Levers"
  },
  "TOPIC_25": {
    "name": "Incomplete Contracts",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_25\nTOPIC_NAME: Incomplete Contracts\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 77, 80, 81, 82, 84\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 83, 85\n\n2. Core ideas:\n“It is typically impossible to write a contract that explicitly specifies what each party should do in every possible future state of the world.”\nContracts are inherently incomplete because:\nfuture events cannot be fully predicted\nnot all contingencies can be specified\ndecision rights must be allocated\nConsequence:\ncontracts leave gaps\nthese gaps create room for renegotiation\nand opportunistic behaviour (“self-interest with guile”)\nHold-up problem:\noccurs when one party makes relationship-specific investment\nthe counterparty can then:\nexploit dependency\nrenegotiate terms\ncapture additional value\nResult:\nunderinvestment\ninefficiency\nrisk of value destruction\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/06/relational-governance-in-outsourcing-contracts.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/hart-lecture.pdf\n5. Practical value:\nYou cannot “fix” incompleteness → you must design for it\nKey procurement implications:\ndefine decision rights clearly\nuse:\ngovernance mechanisms\nescalation paths\nchange management clauses\nmitigate hold-up risk:\navoid over-dependence\ndiversify suppliers where possible\nstructure contracts to balance power\nalign incentives → reduce motivation for opportunistic behaviour\n\nEXAMPLES:\nBuyer invests in specialized equipment\nSupplier later raises prices → exploits dependency\nResult as per the Game Theory:\nbuyer cannot recover the investment\nsupplier may capture short-term additional value\nboth parties suffer from anyone’s opportunistic behaviour.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_23 — Information Asymmetry; TOPIC_24 — Types of Negotiations and Game Theory; TOPIC_22 — Sourcing Value Levers"
  },
  "TOPIC_26": {
    "name": "Relational Contracts",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_26\nTOPIC_NAME: Relational Contracts\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 78, 79, 80, 81, 82, 84\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 83, 85\n\n2. Core ideas:\nContracts exist on a spectrum:\nDiscrete (formal) contracts\nRelational contracts\nRelational contracts:\nbased on trust, cooperation, and long-term relationship\nnot fully specified in formal terms\nrely on mutual understanding and ongoing interaction\nKey contrast:\nFormal contracts → “fixed everything”\nRelational contracts → “shared risk”\nApplicable where:\nuncertainty is high\noutcomes cannot be fully defined upfront\ncontinuous adaptation is required\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/06/relational-governance-in-outsourcing-contracts.html\n4. External:\nhttps://golab.bsg.ox.ac.uk/the-basics/relational-contracting/\n5. Practical value:\nRelational contracts are a response to incomplete contracts\nUse when:\nhigh complexity\nlong-term dependency\ninnovation / transformation scope\nKey mechanisms:\nshared objectives\nflexibility in execution\ncontinuous negotiation\ngovernance instead of strict control\nCritical implication:\nYou cannot manage complex IT services with purely rigid (formal) contracts\nProcurement role is to build relationship + governance, not just legal structure\n\nEXAMPLES:\nComplex IT outsourcing\nrequires ongoing adjustments\nInnovation / transformation program\ncannot be fully specified upfront\nRelational contracts manage uncertainty through trust and governance — not through completeness.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_24 — Types of Negotiations and Game Theory"
  },
  "TOPIC_27": {
    "name": "Technology Procurement Framework",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_27\nTOPIC_NAME: Technology Procurement Framework\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 16, 37, 40, 52\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 52\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThe Technology Procurement Framework separates procurement cost action into two sequential levels:\n\nLevel 1 — Demand Management\nDemand must be understood and challenged before commercial negotiation.\n\nWithin Demand Management:\n• Cost Management — make demand, usage, ownership and spend visible and controllable.\n• Cost Optimization — remove unnecessary demand and improve the efficiency of the remaining requirement.\n\nLevel 2 — Commercial Negotiation\nOnce the demand baseline has been controlled and optimized, procurement applies Commercial Levers to improve the economics and contractual position.\n\nThe sequence is:\nUnderstand and control demand → optimize demand/consumption → apply commercial levers → negotiate the deal.\n\nCost Management answers:\n• What are we using?\n• What are we spending?\n• Who owns the demand?\n• What should be budgeted, monitored or controlled?\n\nCost Optimization answers:\n• Can the same business need be met with less?\n• Can unused, duplicated or inefficient demand be removed?\n• Can capacity, licences, models or service levels be right-sized?\n\nCommercial Levers answer:\n• How can the optimized requirement be bought on better terms?\n• What price, discount, commitment, support, renewal, exit and data terms should be negotiated?\n\n3. Blog:\nN/A\n\n4. External:\nN/A — this is the course's procurement framework.\n\n5. Practical value:\nDo not negotiate against unmanaged demand.\nA supplier discount applied to unnecessary consumption, shelfware or oversized commitments does not create real procurement value.\n\nCloud application — slide 37:\nCost Management:\n• spend visibility by account, workload, service and region\n• budgets, alerts, forecasts and anomaly monitoring\n• tagging, cost centres, showback/chargeback\n• visibility of non-production, DR, backup and data-transfer spend\n\nCost Optimization:\n• rightsizing compute, databases and storage\n• stopping idle resources and scheduling non-production environments\n• autoscaling, serverless and storage tiering where appropriate\n• removal of orphaned resources and redesign of expensive workloads\n\nCommercial Levers:\n• commitment level and reallocation flexibility\n• discount coverage, including future services and marketplace spend where applicable\n• support and professional-service pricing\n• exit/data rights, SLA structure and hidden-cost exposure\n\nSaaS application — slide 40:\nCost Management:\n• application, licence, tier and add-on inventory\n• assigned vs active vs genuinely required users\n• spend by application, business unit and user\n• renewal calendar, utilisation and tier distribution\n\nCost Optimization:\n• reclaim inactive or duplicate licences\n• downgrade unnecessary premium tiers\n• eliminate overlapping applications and unused add-ons\n• right-size renewal quantities\n\nCommercial Levers:\n• volume bands and ramp schedules\n• price protection and renewal caps\n• true-down, transfer and reallocation rights\n• future-module discount coverage\n• support, services, exit and data portability\n\nAI application — slide 52:\nCost Management:\n• inventory use cases, users, models and workload owners\n• track token, request, compute and storage consumption\n• quotas, approval thresholds, budgets and alerts\n• team-level showback/chargeback\n\nCost Optimization:\n• match model to task complexity\n• shorten prompts and remove unnecessary context\n• cache repeated outputs and batch/reuse requests where appropriate\n• eliminate unnecessary calls before scaling\n\nCommercial Levers:\n• token/unit pricing and volume tiers\n• committed-spend flexibility across models\n• future-model, support and professional-service terms\n• data-use, ownership, IP, portability and exit rights\n\nEXAMPLES:\n• SaaS: reclaim unused licences before negotiating the renewal discount.\n• Cloud: right-size and remove idle resources before committing to a multi-year spend level.\n• AI: validate the use case and token demand before accepting a large consumption commitment.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_01 — Software Inflation; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services; TOPIC_17 — SaaS Pricing Models; TOPIC_22 — Sourcing Value Levers\nASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE: Demand Management; Cost Management; Cost Optimization; Commercial Levers; Commercial Negotiation"
  },
  "TOPIC_28": {
    "name": "Transaction Cost Economics",
    "content": "TOPIC_ID: TOPIC_28\nTOPIC_NAME: Transaction Cost Economics\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 41\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: None\n\nCore ideas:\nTransaction Cost Economics explains whether an economic exchange should use the market, a hybrid arrangement or internal hierarchy.\nA transaction creates search and information costs, policy and contracting costs, and policing and enforcement costs.\nAsset specificity measures how much an investment loses value outside the relationship.\nUncertainty concerns demand, technology and partner behaviour.\nFrequency describes how often the transaction occurs.\n\nGovernance model shown on the slide:\nMarket / horizontal outsourcing relies mainly on price and corresponds to low asset specificity, low uncertainty and high frequency.\nHybrid governance relies on trust through a long-term contract or alliance and corresponds to medium asset specificity, low or medium uncertainty and medium or high frequency.\nHierarchy / vertical insourcing relies on authority and corresponds to high asset specificity, high uncertainty and low frequency.\n\nAuthors:\nRonald Coase introduced the role of transaction costs in explaining the boundary of the firm.\nOliver Williamson developed Transaction Cost Economics and comparative governance.\n\nPractical value:\nDefine the economic exchange before choosing make or buy.\nIdentify the transaction activities and their costs.\nUse the transaction characteristics to select a governance mechanism.\nKeep the governance choice separate from the solution form: COTS, customise or build.\n\nExternal:\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/1991/coase/lecture/\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/williamson_lecture.pdf\nhttps://www.coase.org/publications/2022menardshirleyadvancedintrotonie.pdf\nhttps://www.investopedia.com/terms/t/transactioncosts.asp\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_14 — COTS vs SDLC; TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_26 — Relational Contracts"
  }
};

