    "title": "2025–2026 SaaS Market Trends and Challenges",
    "text": "ITPF — Slide 7 Draft v3\nSession 1 · Setting the Scene\n2025–2026 SaaS Market Trends and Challenges\nMarket growth\nPricing and economics\nOwnership and waste\nBuying cadence\n$316bn → $1.5tn\nSaaS market: $316bn in 2025, up from $266bn in 2024 (+19% YoY), and projected to reach $1.5tn by 2034.\n$20.3bn → $91.2bn\nAI-as-a-Service market from 2025 to 2030, projected at 35% YoY growth.\n+75% YoY\nGrowth in spending on AI-native apps, where AI is the core functionality.\n9% EBITDA\nMedian SaaS EBITDA margin in 2025, with 26% growth; median net margin 1.5–2% and gross margins around 80%.\nRule of 40\nEBITDA + growth ≥40% indicates a healthy balance of growth and profitability.\nBusiness ownership\nThe business owned 70% of SaaS spend and 51% of apps in 2025.\nIT ownership\nIT owned 26% of SaaS spend and 16% of apps in 2025.\n$3.1tn\nGlobal spending on digital transformation in 2026; roughly 70% of these investments fail to meet their goals.\nShadow IT\n33% of SaaS apps remain shadow IT; 60% of builders have built something outside IT oversight in the past year.\n3.4%\nof employees expense software cost (down from 7%); this represents 3.7% of spend (up from 1%).\n36–50%\nof SaaS licences remain unused or underused against industry-recommended levels (shelfware).\n23 apps\non average (8% of total) incur multichannel spend.\n7 redundant apps\nper function, including AI apps, online training, project management and team collaboration.\n50%\nof SaaS companies included AI features in their portfolios in 2025.\n+9%\nAverage SaaS price increase when contracts auto-renew.\n58% / 79% / 77%\nVendors that raised prices / customers that noticed an increase / customers reporting unexpected post-negotiation cost.\n57%\nof SaaS vendors hide pricing from public view.\n16.4%\nSoftware inflation in June 2026, compared with US CPI inflation of 3.5% over the same 12-month period.\n70% by 2027\nof the top 20 SaaS vendors by revenue will offer consumption-based pricing for part of their portfolio.\n6 new apps/month\nAverage purchasing cycle: 100 days.\n4.7 renewals/month\nAverage renewal cycle: 60 days.\n77%\nof contracts are for one year only.\n$4,830\nAverage SaaS spend per employee in 2025 (+21% YoY), with 275 apps per company.\nSelect each tab to review the full market evidence.\nSources\nClose\nSource links retained from the course slide\nhttps://newsroom.taylorandfrancisgroup.com/costly-business-overhauls-are-not-needed-to-embrace-new-digital-technologies-according-to-specialist/\nZylo's 2026 SaaS Management Index\nhttps://www.vertice.one/insights/saas-inflation-rate\nhttps://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/14/consumer-price-index-inflation-report-june-2026.html\nhttps://www.vertice.one/reports/beyond-the-numbers-report-thank-you\nhttps://www.highalpha.com/saas-benchmarks\nhttps://softwareequity.com/research/annual-saas-report\nhttps://aventis-advisors.com/saas-valuation-multiples/\nhttps://www.vertice.one/l/saas-inflation-index-report\nhttps://www.statista.com/outlook/tmo/public-cloud/software-as-a-service/worldwide\nhttps://www.forbes.com/sites/steveandriole/2021/03/25/3-main-reasons-why-big-technology-projects-fail---why-many-companies-should-just-never-do-them/\nhttps://productiv.com/state-of-saas/\nhttps://www.fortunebusinessinsights.com/digital-transformation-market-104878\nhttps://www.saas-capital.com/research/private-saas-company-growth-rate-benchmarks/\nhttps://www.marketsandmarkets.com/Market-Reports/artificial-intelligence-ai-as-a-service-market-121842268.html",
