  "26": {
    "title": "Integrated Procurement Planning",
    "text": "ITPF — Slide 26 Draft — Integrated Procurement Planning\nIntegrated Procurement Planning\nSection 2\nAligning procurement activities with the IT Service Lifecycle\nSession 2\nIntegrated Procurement Planning\nProcurement activities must be aligned with the IT Service Lifecycle.\nWe move from sourcing strategy into integrated delivery planning, where procurement, project management and service lifecycle decisions connect.\n©",
    "topicIds": [
      "TOPIC_07",
      "TOPIC_08"
    ]
  },
  "27": {
    "title": "Business-driven ITIL® Service Lifecycle",
    "text": "ITPF — Slide 27 Draft v3 — Business-driven ITIL Service Lifecycle\nSession 2 Integrated Procurement Planning\nBusiness-driven ITIL® Service Lifecycle\nProcurement supports a service lifecycle, not a one-off technical purchase.\nBusiness input\nBusiness and customer requirements\nStarts Service Strategy\nStrategic input\nStrategies and policies\nResources and constraints\nService level expectations\nDefines the strategic service direction\nDesign outputs\nSolution designs\nArchitectures and standards\nService Design Packages\nTested solution plans\nMoves into Service Transition and delivery\nITIL service lifecycle\nService Design\nTranslate requirements into\nservice solutions\nService\nTransition\nBuild, test and\ndeploy changes\nService Operation\nRun and support\nlive services\nContinual\nService\nImprovement\nMeasure, learn\nand optimize\nService\nStrategy\nDefine value, demand\nand sourcing direction\nBusiness requirements feed Service Strategy, which then sets policies, constraints and service level expectations before Service Design begins.\nOperational output\nOperational services\nTransition plans\nKnowledge updated\nService catalogue and portfolio\nProduced mainly by Operation and Transition\nProcurement implication\nSource integrated services, not isolated buys\nPlan for implementation and operation\nInclude improvement actions and lifecycle cost\nExample: Printing-as-a-Service\nSourcing decisions affect the full lifecycle\nDiscussion: where does procurement influence the lifecycle most — strategy, design, transition or operation?\n©",
    "topicIds": [
      "TOPIC_09"
    ]
  },
  "28": {
    "title": "Project Management Cycle",
    "text": "ITPF — Slide 28 Draft — Project Management Cycle\nSession 2 Integrated Procurement Planning\nProject Management Cycle\nSourcing integrated IT solutions requires project-management discipline.\nInitiation\nDevelop the business case\nIdentify scope\nIdentify project stakeholders\n1\n→\nPlanning\nCreate the project plan\nGather resources\nEstimate the budget\n2\n→\nExecution\nEngage resources\nMonitor quality of work\nManage the budget\n3\n→\nClosure\nAnalyse project and team results\nDocument project closure\nAccount for used and unused budget\n4\n→\nProcurement planning must be synchronized with the project cycle, not added after the technical scope is already fixed.\n©",
    "topicIds": [
      "TOPIC_09"
    ]
  },
