  "74": {
    "title": "Principal–Agent Problem: Aligning Incentives",
    "text": "Slide 74 — Principal-Agent Problem and Incentive Alignment\nSession 5 Source-to-Contract\nPrincipal–Agent Problem: Aligning Incentives\nScenario\nThe company hires an IT consultant to select a supplier. The consultant receives a fixed fee of\n£20,000\n.\nA\nSelect Supplier A\nThe company generates\n£200,000\nof additional business benefit.\nB\nSelect Supplier B\nThe company suffers a\n£50,000\nbusiness loss, while the consultant receives a\n£40,000\nsupplier commission.\nInformation asymmetry creates moral hazard: under the basic contract, the consultant earns more by selecting Supplier B.\nPayoffs after consultant compensation\nCompany values are\nnet payoffs\n: business outcome less fees paid to the consultant.\nContract\nSupplier A\n(best choice)\nSupplier B\n(commission)\nBasic contract\n£20k fixed fee\nConsultant:\n£20,000\nCompany:\n£180,000 net\nConsultant:\n£60,000\nCompany:\n−£70,000 net\nPerformance-based\n£20k + 25% of benefit\nConsultant:\n£70,000\nBEST RESPONSE\nCompany:\n£130,000 net\nConsultant:\n£60,000\nCompany:\n−£70,000 net\n1. Basic contract\nSupplier B pays the consultant more: £60k versus £20k.\n2. Performance contract\nSupplier A now pays the consultant more: £70k versus £60k.\n3. Aligned outcome\nThe consultant’s best response now supports the company’s objective.\nGame-theory lesson:\nthe performance-based contract changes the consultant’s rational choice from Supplier B (£60k) to Supplier A (£70k), aligning the agent’s interests with the principal’s objective.\nWhich contract design makes the desired outcome rational?\nMisaligned incentives explain opportunistic behaviour; incomplete contracts explain why the agreement cannot fully prevent it.\n© CIPS. Simplified payoff example.",
    "topicIds": [
      "TOPIC_23",
      "TOPIC_24"
    ]
  },
