  "77": {
    "title": "Incomplete Contracts and the Hold-up Problem",
    "text": "Slide 77 — Incomplete Contracts and the Hold-up Problem\nSession 5 Source-to-Contract\nIncomplete Contracts and the Hold-up Problem\nCore concept\nNumeric game\nWhy the risk appears\nA buyer makes a relationship-specific investment because cooperation is expected to create additional profit.\nOnce the investment is sunk, switching becomes more expensive. The supplier may then have an incentive to exploit the buyer’s new dependency.\n1\nSpecific investment\nThe buyer invests in equipment or capability with limited value outside the relationship.\n2\nDependency\nThe investment changes the buyer’s switching economics after commitment.\n3\nOpportunistic repricing\nThe supplier attempts to capture more value after the buyer is locked in.\n4\nValue destruction\nIf the buyer exits, the buyer strands investment and the supplier loses future business.\nHow procurement can reduce the risk\nVertical integration / insourcing\nOwn the critical capability where dependency on one supplier is unacceptable.\nLong-term contractual protection\nFix critical pricing rules, change mechanisms, decision rights and exit protections before the investment is made.\nRevenue or risk sharing\nAlign incentives so both parties benefit from preserving the cooperative surplus.\nRelational governance\nUse transparency, escalation and joint decision-making where every future state cannot be written into the contract.\nThe key risk is not merely a transfer of value through a higher price. Opportunism can trigger an exit decision that destroys value for\nboth\nparties.\nGame assumptions\nAnnual business volume\n£1.0m\nRemaining commercial horizon\n3 years\nP\nBuyer profit increase from investment\n£150k / yr\nV\nBuyer specialised investment\n£180k once\nR\nSupplier opportunistic price increase\n£220k / yr\nB\nSupplier annual business margin at risk\n£150k / yr\nFor the exercise, B assumes a 15% supplier contribution margin on the £1.0m annual business volume. Payoffs below are incremental to the original contract economics.\nAfter the buyer invests, what should each party do — and what happens to value if the supplier exploits the lock-in?\nStage 1 — Buyer invests V = £180k.\nThe investment can create P = £150k additional profit per year for the remaining 3-year relationship.\nScenario 1 · Supplier keeps agreed pricing\nCooperation continues for all three years.\nBuyer = 3P − V = 3×150 − 180\nBuyer:\n+£270k\nSupplier:\nbaseline economics retained\nStage 2 · Supplier raises price by R = £220k/year\nThe increase comes only after the buyer’s specialised investment is sunk.\nBuyer must now compare staying for 3 years with switching after Year 1.\nStage 3 — Buyer response to the opportunistic increase\nScenario 2 · Accept increase and stay\nBuyer = 3(P − R) − V\n= 3×(150 − 220) − 180 =\n−£390k\nSupplier incremental gain = 3R =\n+£660k\nShort-term value is transferred to the supplier, but the buyer’s three-year economics become negative.\nScenario 3 · Absorb Year 1, then switch\nBuyer = P − V − R\n= 150 − 180 − 220 =\n−£250k\nSupplier = R − 2B\n= 220 − 300 =\n−£80k\nSwitching is less damaging to the buyer than staying (−£250k vs −£390k), but it strands the investment and costs the supplier two years of business margin.\nGame-theory lesson:\ncooperation gives the buyer\n+£270k\n. Opportunistic repricing makes staying economically irrational; the rational response is to switch even though switching still produces a loss. The result is a destructive branch where\nbuyer −£250k and supplier −£80k\n. Opportunism therefore destroys the cooperative surplus that both parties could have preserved.\n© CIPS. Simplified classroom game; the numbers illustrate relationship-specific investment, post-investment repricing and value destruction after switching.",
    "topicIds": [
      "TOPIC_25"
    ]
  },
